12. Software Terms

12.0 Why This Section Exists

Creative Commons advises against using its licenses for software (https://creativecommons.org/faq/#can-i-apply-a-creative-commons-license-to-software). CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 does not address source code, object code, network use or linking, and it grants no patent rights (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0, Section 2(b)(2)). This Section applies to Software the same principles that govern all other Content: attribution, non-commercial use, share-alike, the restrictions in Section 2.2, the prior art requirement in Section 5, and separate commercial licensing under Section 10.7. It states them in terms written for software. For Software, this Section and the Supplemental Terms (Sections 2–10) replace CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 as the base license (Section 1.1). In any conflict between this Section and another Section as applied to Software, this Section prevails.

12.1 Definitions

"Software" means all Content that is computer code in any form: source code, object code, executables, libraries, packages, scripts, notebooks, build and configuration files, infrastructure-as-code, machine-checkable proofs and formal specifications (for example Lean, Coq or Isabelle files), and the documentation, tests, examples and data files distributed with them.
"Source Code" means the preferred form of the Software for making modifications, including the build scripts and configuration needed to build, install and run it.
"Object Code" means any form of the Software other than Source Code, including compiled, bundled, minified, packaged or container forms.
"Modification" means any change to a file of the Software, and any new file that contains a substantial portion of a file of the Software. A separate file that You write, and that does not copy code from the Software, is not a Modification merely because it calls, imports, links to or communicates with the Software.
"Covered Files" means the files of the Software and all Modifications.
"Larger Work" means a work that combines Covered Files with files that are not Covered Files.
"Distribute" means to make a copy of the Software, a Modification or a Larger Work available to any person or entity other than You, by any means, including publishing a repository, a package, a container image or a download.
"Network Use" means letting any person or entity other than You interact with the Software, a Modification or a Larger Work remotely through a computer network.
"Contribution" means any code, documentation or other material that You submit to Licensor for inclusion in the Software, including through a pull request, patch or issue attachment.

12.2 Copyright License Grant for Software

Subject to this Agreement, Licensor grants You a worldwide, royalty-free, non-exclusive, non-sublicensable license to use, run, copy, study, modify and Distribute the Software and Modifications, in Source Code or Object Code form, solely for non-commercial purposes as defined in Sections 2.1, 2.4 and 12.3. Each person who receives the Software from You receives this license directly from Licensor.

12.3 Commercial Use of Software Requires a Separate Agreement

Any use of the Software that generates money is Commercial Use and requires a separate written agreement with Licensor under Section 10.7. This includes any use of the Software, a Modification or a Larger Work that earns revenue for, saves money for, or forms part of the operations of a for-profit person or organization. Without limitation, Commercial Use includes:

(a) selling, renting, licensing or sublicensing the Software, or charging for access to it, for downloads, for installation, for hosting or for support;

(b) offering the Software, or functionality substantially provided by it, as or within a paid product, a hosted service (software-as-a-service), an API, an application or a platform, including a free product or service that is monetized through advertising, data collection, subscriptions, upselling or investment;

(c) using the Software to produce deliverables, results, analyses or services for which You or anyone else is paid, including consulting, contracting and freelance work;

(d) any use by or for a for-profit entity, including internal operations, research and development, evaluation, testing and employee training (Section 2.4(g));

(e) embedding the Software in, or bundling it with, hardware, devices or other products that are sold or leased;

(f) using the Software, its Source Code or its outputs to train, fine-tune or evaluate a commercial artificial intelligence or code-generation model, system or service (Sections 2.1, 2.2(e) and 6.3 apply to Software in full); and

(g) any other use in which the Software contributes to revenue, investment value, market position or cost savings for any person or organization.

The following are non-commercial uses of the Software, consistent with Section 2.4: personal use by an individual that earns no money; teaching and study; academic and scientific research whose results are published or otherwise made publicly available; and use by a registered non-profit organization, an educational institution or a government body for its public mission. Grants, donations and tuition that fund these activities are not revenue from the Software, provided that the Software is not itself sold, licensed, hosted for a fee or made the paid component of any product, service or deliverable. Any of these users that later uses the Software in a way described in (a) to (g) needs a separate agreement for that use.

Licensor claims no ownership of the data You put into the Software or of the results it produces for You. The restriction in this Section applies to how the Software is used, not to who owns its outputs.

12.4 Reciprocity — ShareAlike for Software

Section 12.4 replaces Sections 4.1 and 4.2 for Software. If You Distribute the Software, a Modification or a Larger Work:

(a) Same license. You must Distribute the Covered Files only under this Agreement (QNFO-ULA v2.1 or a later version issued by Licensor). CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 alone is not a compatible license for Software.

(b) Source Code. You must make the complete Source Code of the Covered Files You Distribute available to every recipient, free of charge, for as long as You Distribute them. You can include it with the distribution, or provide a written notice with a working link to it.

(c) Notices. You must keep intact every copyright notice, SPDX header (Section 3.1 and Appendix C), the LICENSE file and any NOTICE file. You must also state in the changed files, with the date, that You modified them (Section 3.2).

(d) Larger Works. Files in a Larger Work that are not Covered Files may be licensed under terms of Your choosing. Those terms may not purport to grant any right in the Covered Files, and the Covered Files in the Larger Work stay under this Agreement. The non-commercial restriction in Section 12.3 applies to every use of the Larger Work in which the Covered Files are used.

(e) Network Use. If You modify the Software and make it available for Network Use, You must offer each user the Source Code of Your Modification under this Agreement. Do this through a prominent link in the user interface or in the API documentation.

(f) No additional restrictions. You may not impose further restrictions on any recipient's exercise of the rights granted by this Agreement, and You may not use technical measures to prevent recipients from modifying or rebuilding the Covered Files (Section 4.3).

